.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 24, 2026
.Dt ADJKERNTZ 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm adjkerntz
.Nd tell the kernel the local time zone's offset from UTC
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl a
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The system clock keeps UTC.
A few on-disk formats store local time instead, the FAT file system's timestamps
among them, so the kernel needs the local zone's offset to convert:
.Nm
sets it.
.Pp
The machine's hardware (CMOS) clock keeps UTC too, unless
.Pa /etc/wall_cmos_clock
exists: then it keeps local time, as Windows keeps it, and
.Xr rc 8
sets the
.Va machdep.wall_cmos_clock
variable to 1 before running
.Nm .
Once both are known, the kernel takes the offset in effect then off the time it
read from the hardware clock at boot, once; a later offset, at a change to or
from daylight saving time, doesn't move the system clock, and keeping the
hardware clock in local time across it is the other system's job.
.Pp
The option is as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a
Read the offset in effect now from
.Pa /etc/localtime
and set the
.Va kern.utc_offset
.Xr sysctl 8
variable, in minutes east of UTC.
Without
.Pa /etc/localtime
the offset is 0.
The
.Ev TZ
environment variable is ignored: the kernel's offset is the system's, not a
user's.
.El
.Pp
.Xr rc 8
runs
.Nm
.Fl a
at boot, before any file system is checked or mounted.
The system
.Xr crontab 5
runs it again at one minute past every hour, so the offset follows daylight
saving time, and
.Xr tzsetup 8
runs it after changing the zone.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/wall_cmos_clock -compact
.It Pa /etc/localtime
The local time zone.
.It Pa /etc/wall_cmos_clock
If present, the hardware clock keeps local time.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tzfile 5 ,
.Xr mount_msdosfs 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
.Xr tzsetup 8
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
command appeared in
.Fx 1.0 ,
where it also adjusts a hardware clock kept in local time; here the kernel
corrects the time it reads instead, and the hardware clock is never written.
The
.Va kern.utc_offset
variable is
.Ox Ns 's
name; there it is the hardware clock's offset.
.Sh BUGS
Times are converted with the offset in effect now, not the one in effect at the
time converted: a file written in summer shows an hour off in winter, as it does
on
.Fx
and Windows.
